<Base title="About" description="About the author and this site." person>
  <Prose>
    <p class="font-(family-name:--font-ui) uppercase tracking-widest text-(--color-muted) text-xs">About</p>
    <h1>Graham Wright</h1>
    <p>
      I work in nonprofit and cultural organizations, helping teams use technology, creative,
      and communications systems to serve their missions better. I'm currently the Director
      of Content and Digital Services at the
      <a href="https://www.bso.org">Boston Symphony Orchestra</a>, which also includes the
      Boston Pops, Tanglewood Music Festival, and Symphony Hall.
    </p>
    <h2>How I got here</h2>
    <p>
      Everything I do now draws on a mix of experiences that might not seem connected on
      paper, but they shape how I think and how I lead. I came to Boston to pursue a PhD
      in chemistry at MIT and discovered a music community that became my home. Over time
      I left the lab, but not the laboratory mindset.
    </p>
    <p>
      I built an early career as a scientific conference producer. In the lab, I'd been
      focused on research, teaching, and ideas. Conference production showed me how much
      else it takes to build a community around an idea: marketing, communications, sales,
      operations. And it all came together when I co-founded the
      <a href="https://www.bostonsociety.org/">Boston Society</a>, a nonprofit conference
      organization. It was my first time on a founding team.
    </p>
    <p>
      Meanwhile, I spent more than a decade as a working musician, a classical vocalist
      performing opera and concert repertoire in and around Boston, including with Boston
      Lyric Opera, Opera Boston, and the Boston Pops.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://opusaffair.org">Opus Affair</a> came from both worlds. I founded it
      as a monthly gathering of people interested in the arts (artists, administrators,
      audiences) focused on the social experience around performance, not the performance
      itself. The premise was that more people cared about the arts than recognized each
      other yet, and that showing up regularly would build a critical mass. It did. Opus
      Affair grew quickly, gathering thousands of members and partnering with over a hundred
      arts and cultural organizations across Boston. It became a real community, one that
      helped organizations find new audiences and helped people find each other.
    </p>
    <p>
      During the early Opus Affair years, I also took on event production and leadership
      roles at organizations including the Museum of Science, WGBH, and
      <a href="https://www.afarcry.org/">A Far Cry</a>, where I served as interim executive
      director during a transformational period for the ensemble. Seeing those organizations
      up close shaped my understanding of what they needed. That perspective, combined with
      the momentum of the community, led my decision to grow Opus Affair into a boutique
      agency.
    </p>
    <p>
      Our partners needed more than events. They needed digital and creative support. What
      started as a Boston community became a national presence, driving impact behind the
      scenes through digital transformation and platform work.
    </p>
    <p>
      At the BSO, those threads come together. I lead a team that is often described as
      our internal creative and digital agency. We support every part of the institution:
      artistic, education, fundraising, operations. Our work spans technology and data, creative and editorial,
      and the cross-functional processes that keep a complex organization moving. What I'm
      focused on now is advancing our communications technology and data roadmap, with AI
      as a major part of that work. And on telling the stories that connect our audiences
      to what we have to offer.
    </p>
  </Prose>
</Base>
